After that first checkpoint, the real project work starts. You’ll still have most of the implementation left, but it becomes incremental rather than confusing.

The remaining roadmap is:

Custom forwarding controller
Make Ryu install actual OpenFlow rules so h1 → s1 → s2 → s4 → h2 works reliably.
NetworkX topology model
Represent switches/links as a graph and compute routes programmatically.
Healthcare traffic classification
Distinguish ECG, ICU, telemedicine, EHR, imaging, CCTV, etc., and assign priorities. Your proposal explicitly centers on life-critical healthcare traffic.
Traffic generation
Generate realistic synthetic flows with ping, iperf, UDP/TCP scripts, including heavy background traffic.
Network monitoring
Poll switch statistics for utilization, packet counts, loss, and congestion state.
Congestion-aware routing
Dynamically reroute traffic away from overloaded links.
Criticality-aware routing
Use different path-cost weights for critical versus non-critical flows.
Braess-paradox module
Before enabling or preferring a new route, estimate whether it would increase critical-flow delay. This is the main novelty in your current proposal.
Priority queues
Give ECG/ICU traffic higher scheduling priority than CCTV/imaging.
Fast failover / backup path
Precompute backup routes so failures don’t require a slow controller recomputation.
Prediction / AI component
Predict congestion or latency from recent network statistics. Keep this lightweight so it doesn’t itself increase latency.
Full MedRoute integration
Combine classifier + monitoring + prediction + route selection + Braess safety + OpenFlow enforcement. This matches your proposed controller pipeline.
Automated simulation experiments
Test normal load, congestion, bursts, link failure, Braess topology, increasing flows, and increasing network size.

